output.design:
Adopt a unit model with one primary organising axis: the boundary at which a thing is independently owned, tested, released or deployed, and allowed to be depended on. Do not use size, number of exports, language, or change frequency alone as unit criteria. A new code unit exists only when it has a distinct consumer-facing contract, lifecycle, runtime/support promise, provenance/control regime, or dependency-direction role. Otherwise keep cohesive code together behind subpath exports.

The resulting top-level layout is:

apps/<product>/ — supported deployable products;
demos/<demo>/ — explicitly unsupported or time-bounded audience demonstrations;
packages/platform/<capability>/ — domain-neutral primitives;
packages/domains/<domain>/<capability>/ — Oak-specific business capabilities;
packages/integrations/<system>/<capability>/ — external-system clients and adapters;
packages/presentation/<capability>/ — reusable UI, tokens, or widget sources;
tools/<purpose>/ — commands, generators, guards, and operations;
evidence/<study>/ — non-shipping research code;
materials/<owner>/<material>/ — non-code data and assets, each with its own provenance/rights declaration.

Every independently built TypeScript unit is a workspace package with an explicit package boundary. Package names describe scope and capability, not an implementation category: for example @oaknational/result, @oaknational/safe-path, @oaknational/curriculum-client, and @oaknational/elasticsearch-retrieval. Directory position expresses its architectural role; package exports express its public contract. The present checkout already provides evidence for this direction: workspace membership distinguishes apps, demos, core packages, libraries, SDKs and agent tools; package manifests consistently carry exports and scripts; Turbo already models generated-output ownership; and the current SDK-codegen manifest demonstrably combines several incompatible boundaries that should be separated.

Use a required manifest extension, validated centrally, for example: "oak": { "unitKind": "platform|domain|integration|presentation|app|demo|tool|evidence|material", "scope": "...", "audience": "public|internal", "support": "supported|demo|research|operator-only", "risk": "normal|security-critical|live-operations", "provenance": "authored|generated|external-byte-preserved|curated", "release": "published|workspace-private|not-shipped", "artifactOwner": "package-name-or-null" }. Put only independently varying facts here; derive dependency facts from the graph rather than duplicating them.

Generated material is never a peer of its generator by convention alone: the producer declares outputs, trigger, and source inputs; a consumer declares the producer-owned artifact it embeds. Curated, vendor, and rights-constrained material gets a small local machine-readable rights/provenance file and is not silently swept into a code package merely because it is nearby.

Enforcement is mechanical: workspace-pattern validation checks top-level placement and manifest completeness; dependency-cruiser (or equivalent) derives and rejects forbidden edges; package exports reject deep imports; Turbo owns task/output edges; CI checks regenerated output matches declared inputs; and policy tests enforce support, release, risk, provenance, licensing, and artifact-embedding constraints. Allowed high-level direction is app/demo/tool/evidence → domain/integration/presentation/platform; domain → integration/platform; integration/presentation → platform; platform → platform only. Tools may use capabilities but no product application may be imported by a tool. Evidence may use capabilities but must not be a production dependency. Demos must not become dependencies of supported apps. A security-critical platform package has stricter test and review policy, but remains platform rather than becoming a separate architectural layer.

A unit-placement decision asks: (1) Who consumes it and what contract do they receive? (2) Is it deployed, published, shipped, operator-only, demo-only, or research-only? (3) Is its scope platform-generic, domain-specific, or tied to an external system? (4) What provenance and editability govern its contents? (5) Who produces any built artifact and who merely embeds it? (6) What dependency directions are permitted? (7) What compatibility, support, rights, security, and live-system obligations attach to change?

Keeperless unit grid

The estate is organised as a graph of governed workspace nodes, not as one deep subject taxonomy. A unit is the smallest independently governable body and the largest body that can truthfully share one capability, accountable human owner, rights class, byte authority, renewal event, exposure, support promise, execution contract and assurance regime. Every code, data or asset unit is a pnpm workspace with one package manifest; cross-unit use occurs only through declared package or artefact edges.

The design answers: What single capability does this own? How is it consumed? Who is accountable for it, and what may determine its bytes? What event legitimately replaces it? Under what rights may it travel? Who may receive it? What support promise applies? Who invokes it, what does failure mean, and what live authority can it receive? Which unattended checks protect it? Where does it sit in the dependency graph, and is a genericity claim true?

Mandatory split conditions are: different capability or consumer contract; different human accountability; incompatible rights; different byte authority; different machine-renewal event or replacement mechanism; different exposure, support or release promise; different invocation, failure or privilege contract; different assurance obligations; or a dependency cut needed to keep generic mechanism independent of product policy. A tracked generated output consumed by another unit is separate from its producer. Ordinary untracked build output remains an implementation detail.

Same provider, credential, owner, refresh rate, change rhythm, directory shape or commit does not make things one unit. Neither size nor symbol count decides a boundary. Multi-unit commits remain valid atomic changes and are checked using the affected graph.

The physical layout uses only consumption form as its primary axis:
units/programs/<domain-capability-role> for servers, CLIs, apps, generators, hooks, jobs, studies and live operations;
units/libraries/<domain-capability-role> for imported TypeScript APIs;
units/data/<domain-capability-role> for structured snapshots, schemas, corpora and curated records;
units/assets/<domain-capability-role> for byte-consumed bundles, CSS, images and preserved vendor payloads.

Illustrative first-hour map:
programs: curriculum-mcp-server, search-cli, two *-demo applications, interactive-widget, agent-interactive-command, agent-commit-hook, agent-scheduled-job, repo-state-validator, curriculum-spec-refresh, curriculum-bulk-refresh, curriculum-type-codegen, curriculum-corpus-mine, design-token-build, *-study and *-live-ops;
libraries: error-primitives, path-safety, graph-data, curriculum-client, search-retrieval, observability-api, observability-<vendor>-adapter, design-system, curriculum-spec-types, search-contracts and curriculum-bulk-schemas;
data: curriculum-interface-spec-snapshot, curriculum-bulk-dataset, curriculum-mined-corpus, curriculum-reference and design-token-source;
assets: brand-assets, <component>-vendor-bytes, <component>-authored-styles, interactive-widget-bundle and design-token-css.

Each package manifest contains standard name, private, license, exports, files and bin fields plus an estateUnit record containing capability, human owner, custody, renewal, visibility, delivery, support, execution, assurance and explicit non-import relations. Dependencies describe consumption; produces, embeds, fetches and usesCredential make generators, assets and privileged operations visible in the same graph. Secrets are never unit contents: executable roots receive named capabilities at runtime.

Unattended enforcement consists of one unit validator plus graph-derived Turbo tasks. It rejects unowned payload files, mixed rights, cross-unit relative or deep imports, undeclared dependencies, cycles, invalid role edges, hand edits to machine-owned bytes, unreproducible outputs, vendor hash drift, private leakage from public packages, forbidden production reachability, browser-to-secret reachability and false genericity claims. A fast affected-subgraph check serves commit-time use; full CI checks the whole graph. A generated units:explain command must show a visiting engineer the owner, purpose, rights, custody, replacement command, producer, consumers, runtime roots, support, gates, privileges and the reason every edge is permitted.
